That person is called a worldling (puthujjana) because of reasons such as generating many and various defilements. Regarding this, the Blessed One taught:

'They are called worldlings because they generate many defilements;
they are called worldlings because they possess many self-identity views (sakkāya-diṭṭhi) not yet abandoned by the Path;
they are called worldlings because they look up to the faces of many teachers;
they are called worldlings because they are not yet released from all the many destinations (gati);
they are called worldlings because they perform many various volitional formations (kamma);
they are called worldlings because they are carried away by many various floods (ogha);
they are called worldlings because they are tormented by many various heats;
they are called worldlings because they are burned by many various fevers;
they are called worldlings because they are intensely attached, greedy, infatuated, intoxicated, overwhelmed, fettered, entangled, and oppressed by the many five cords of sensual pleasure;
they are called worldlings because they are many times obstructed, hindered, enveloped, shut in, covered, and overturned by the five hindrances (nīvaraṇa).'

Alternatively, one is called a worldling because of being included among the many people who have passed beyond the count, who are turned away from the Dhamma of the Noble Ones, and who practice base conduct. Or, a worldling is one who is distinct and does not associate with the Noble Ones (Ariyas) who are endowed with virtues such as morality (sīla) and learning (suta). Thus, by these two words 'unlearned worldling' (assutavā puthujjana)—

'Two kinds of worldlings were taught by the Buddha, the Kinsman of the Sun: one is the blind worldling (andha-puthujjana), and one is the virtuous worldling (kalyāṇa-puthujjana).'—

among these two kinds of worldlings taught, it should be understood that only the blind worldling is intended here.

'Does not know as it really is' means not knowing according to reality that 'this life-continuum mind (bhavaṅga-citta) is defiled by these adventitious defilements, and it is liberated in this way.' Because of not knowing thus, in the phrase 'there is no mental development (citta-bhāvanā),' it is shown that the absence of mental stability and mental comprehension is taught as 'there is none.'

193:
The child came out from the womb like water from a water-strainer. The people sitting around, though their faces were drenched with tears, began to laugh. The joyful multitude went to inform the prince of the news of the son's birth.

As soon as the prince saw their expressions, he thought, "I believe the words spoken by the Blessed One have been fulfilled." He came and told the princess the Buddha's words. The princess said, "Prince, the meal that allows for life will indeed be an auspicious meal. Go again and invite the Blessed One for seven days." The prince did so. For seven days, they provided a great offering to the Saṅgha headed by the Buddha. Because the child was born while quenching the burning hearts of all his relatives, they named him Prince Sīvali. Because he had stayed in the womb for seven years, Prince Sīvali was capable of enduring all tasks from the time of his birth. On the seventh day, the General of the Dhamma, Venerable Sāriputta, engaged in a pleasant conversation with the prince. The Blessed One also recited this verse in the Dhammapada:

"He who has crossed over this mire of passion, this difficult path, this cycle of rebirth, and this delusion;
Who has crossed over the four floods and reached the other shore of Nibbāna; who is meditative, free from craving, free from doubt;
Who is cooled without clinging—him I call a Brahmin."

Then the Elder said to the prince, "After experiencing such a mass of suffering, is it not proper for you to ordain?" He replied, "Venerable Sir, if I get the opportunity, I will ordain." Suppavāsā, seeing the child talking with the Elder, thought, "What is my son talking about with the General of the Dhamma?" She approached the Elder and asked, "Venerable Sir, what is my son talking about with you?" "He is speaking about the suffering of the womb he experienced, and says he will ordain if you permit him," the Elder replied. "Venerable Sir, it is well; please ordain him," she said. The Elder took the child to the monastery, gave him the meditation on the five skin-parts, and while ordaining him, said, "Sīvali, there is no need for other instructions for you. Just reflect on the suffering you experienced for seven years." He replied, "Venerable Sir, the act of ordaining is your responsibility; as for what I can do, I shall know it." At the moment the first lock of hair was shaved, Prince Sīvali was established in the Fruit of Stream-entry. At the moment the second lock was shaved, in the Fruit of Once-returning. At the moment the third lock was shaved, in the Fruit of Non-returning. The shaving of all the hair and the realization of the Fruit of Arahantship occurred almost simultaneously. From the day he ordained, the four requisites arose for the Saṅgha as they desired. Thus, the story arose here.
